# 16. PERFORMANCE & FIREBASE COST PROTECTION

The new dashboard must not create unnecessary Firebase reads.

Required architecture:

1. One admin-authorized API request.
2. One `orders` one-time snapshot read.
3. One `settings/coupons` one-time snapshot read where needed.
4. One in-memory aggregation pass.
5. Return only aggregate/sanitized output.

Do not:

- Open real-time `.on('value')` listeners.
- Fetch individual orders in a loop.
- Query every coupon independently.
- Repeat the full orders read for each table row.
- Re-read `orders` for every KPI.
- Create a polling loop.
- Auto-refresh every few seconds.
- Load all raw orders into the browser.
- Send full order snapshots to the UI.

Use manual Refresh and explicit filter Apply controls.

The initial version may scan the entire current `/orders` node once per report request, consistent with the existing BI approach, but the aggregation must remain linear and must not perform nested database reads.

If current order volume or RTDB limitations indicate a real performance problem, report measured evidence before introducing a caching or summary-storage architecture.

Do not add new Firebase aggregation counters in this phase.

